<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Court (Germany)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1934–1945</span> · Also known as Volksgerichtshof</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-court-bulgaria">People's Court</a> was a special court set up in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1934 to try treason and other political offences outside the ordinary justice system. Under its president Roland Freisler from 1942 it held show trials, notably of the 20 July plotters and the White Rose students, and passed more than 5,000 death sentences.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-court-germany.jpg" alt="People's Court (Germany)" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UnknownUnknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_151-39-23,_Volksgerichtshof,_Reinecke,_Freisler,_Lautz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
